#include "avrCompat.h"
#include "machineDependent.h"
#include "gamebuinoShim.h"

// Shufflepuck Cafe (AWOT83, GPLv3 -
// github.com/Awot83/Gamebuino-Shufflepuck_cafe).
//
// IMPORTANT finding from reading the real 189-line .ino directly (not
// assumed from the repo's name/description): despite what the repo name
// suggests, this upstream sketch contains NO air-hockey/Pong gameplay code
// at all. The entire real source is: (1) a blocking `gb.titleScreen(F(""),
// intro)` boot screen that shows a logo bitmap with no text, then (2) a
// `loop()` that is nothing but a browsable gallery of 6 animated alien
// portraits from the 1990 "Shufflepuck Cafe" video game (Bar, Nerual,
// Lexan, Visine, Biff, Bejin - each a 3-frame idle-animation bitmap set),
// switched between with LEFT/RIGHT, with A manually stepping the idle
// animation, B jumping back to the first ("bar") screen, and C restarting
// back to the title screen. There is no ball, no paddle, no score, no win
// condition anywhere in this file - it reads as an unfinished intro/
// character-select shell for a game whose actual air-hockey mechanics were
// never committed to this repo. Ported faithfully as exactly that: a
// character gallery, not an invented air-hockey minigame.
//
// Every real `gb.x.y(...)` call site was mechanically rewritten to a plain
// `gbY(...)` function call (see gamePong.c's own header comment for why).
// Upstream's own blocking `gb.titleScreen(F(""), intro)` (called once from
// setup()/mainMenu(), and again - by re-running setup() itself - whenever
// Button C is pressed mid-loop) was converted into an explicit
// SPUCK_STATE_TITLE/SPUCK_STATE_GALLERY state machine, matching gamePong.c's
// own established "blocking title call -> explicit resumable state, C
// returns to it mid-play" precedent (pongUpdatePlay's own C handling) -
// including preserving the fact that, on real hardware, re-running setup()
// does NOT reset any of upstream's own globals (x/y/StatAnim), so a trip
// through the title screen here likewise leaves spuckCharacter/
// spuckAnimTimer/spuckAnimFrame completely untouched.
//
// All 19 real upstream PROGMEM bitmaps (bar/bar2/bar3, nerual/nerual2/
// nerual3, lexan x3, visine x3, biff x3, bejin x3, plus the "intro" logo)
// are drawn via this shim's own `gbDrawBitmap()` primitive (a direct port
// of real `Display::drawBitmap()`). Every real byte was copied from the
// upstream .ino verbatim, one plain `int` per original PROGMEM byte,
// exactly like gbFont5x7's own layout in gamebuinoShim.c. Upstream's own
// byte literals are a mix of `0x`-prefixed hex (most of the character
// portraits) and Arduino's own `B00000000`-style binary literals (the
// "intro" logo only) - neither the hex nor the decimal literals needed any
// change, but every `B`-literal is NOT valid syntax in this project's own
// C dialect (confirmed - only plain decimal/`0x` hex work here) and had to
// be converted. Done with a small Python script (see the orchestrating
// session's own scratchpad, not checked into this repo) that regexes every
// `const byte NAME[] PROGMEM = { ... }` block straight out of the real
// .ino, parses each token as `B`-binary/`0x`-hex/decimal as appropriate,
// and prints the decimal equivalent - a machine-verified conversion rather
// than ~150 bytes converted by hand. Spot-checked against hand computation
// before trusting the rest (e.g. `B00010000`=16, `B00000101`=5,
// `B00000100`=4, `0xFA`=250, `0x38`=56 - all confirmed by independent
// `$((2#...))`/hex arithmetic, matching the script's own output exactly)
// and cross-checked every array's own declared width/height header bytes
// against its real byte count (`ceil(width/8)*height`) - all 19 arrays
// matched exactly (88x48 for every character portrait, 64x36 for the intro
// logo).
//
// spuckDrawCharacter() selects the exact bitmap upstream's own
// `BAR[]`/`NERUAL[]`/`LEXAN[]`/`VISINE[]`/`BIFF[]`/`BEJIN[]` per-character
// arrays-of-3 indexed by `StatAnim` (ported here as spuckCharacter/
// spuckAnimFrame) and draws it via `gbDrawBitmap(0, 0, ...)` - the exact
// coordinates upstream's own `gb.display.drawBitmap(0, 0, ...)` call sites
// used (confirmed by re-reading every real call site in loop() - none use
// rotation/flip, so plain gbDrawBitmap() suffices, gbDrawBitmapRotated()
// is unused here). The title screen draws the real "intro" logo bitmap
// alongside "PRESS A" (upstream's own `gb.titleScreen(F(""), intro)`
// passed an empty title string - the logo bitmap was always the only real
// title content).
//
// The idle-animation timer itself (upstream's own `y`/`StatAnim` globals,
// renamed spuckAnimTimer/spuckAnimFrame) is ported with its exact original
// thresholds (15/30/45/60) and exact y-jump values the A-button handler
// uses (31/46/0) preserved byte-for-byte, even though they read as magic
// numbers - they're genuinely load-bearing for how the idle "blink" cycle
// visibly plays out (frame 0 held for the first ~30 ticks, then a quick
// pulse through frames 1 and 2 before looping), not incidental.
//
// A genuine upstream quirk, preserved exactly rather than "fixed": LEFT
// clamps the character index at a minimum of 1, never 0 - only the B
// button can actually jump to index 0 (the "bar" screen). Manually
// pressing LEFT enough times will never reach it.
//
// No sound (upstream's loop() never calls into gb.sound at all - none
// added), no EEPROM/high-score persistence (nothing upstream to persist -
// there is no score), and gb.pickRandomSeed()/battery.show=false were
// dropped outright (no randomness is used anywhere in this game - it never
// calls random()/arand() - and the battery indicator is purely cosmetic on
// real hardware), matching gamePong.c's own precedent for both.

